sections.s2_technical:
The first case for a Role is compute. When the statement service runs on EC2, you attach an instance profile that wraps a Role. The SDK's default credential provider automatically fetches temporary credentials from the instance metadata endpoint, uses them, and refreshes them before they expire. You write zero credential-handling code.

```java
import software.amazon.awssdk.auth.credentials.DefaultCredentialsProvider;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.S3Client;

// On EC2/Lambda this picks up the Role's temporary credentials automatically.
S3Client s3 = S3Client.builder()
        .credentialsProvider(DefaultCredentialsProvider.create())
        .build();
```

The second case is cross-account access. National Bank of India has a production account and an analytics account. The analytics account must read production's settlement data. Instead of copying production access keys into the analytics account, you create a Role in production whose trust policy allows the analytics account to assume it. The analytics job calls `assumeRole` and gets temporary production credentials scoped to exactly what it needs.

The third case is federation — users logging in through an identity provider or Single Sign-On assume a Role and never get a permanent AWS User at all.

Why never embed long-lived keys: an access key is a two-part secret (key ID and secret key) that does not expire on its own. If it lands in a Git commit, a Docker image layer, a log line, or a stack trace, it is compromised forever until someone notices and rotates it. Attackers scan public repositories for exactly these strings within seconds of a push. Temporary credentials from STS cut the blast radius to the token's lifetime, usually one hour.

A senior nuance: even for local development you should use `aws sso login` or a named profile rather than keys in the code. And when a Role calls another Role, the role chaining caps the session to one hour regardless of the configured maximum.

