sections.s2_technical:
S3 stores objects, not blocks or files on a disk. Each object has a key (its name), data, and metadata, and lives in a bucket with a globally unique name. S3 gives eleven nines of durability by replicating objects across multiple devices and, for most classes, multiple Availability Zones. You access it with simple API calls.

```java
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.S3Client;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.model.*;

S3Client s3 = S3Client.builder().build();
// Upload a statement PDF into the Standard class
s3.putObject(PutObjectRequest.builder()
        .bucket("nbi-statements")
        .key("2026/10/rajesh-SAV2001.pdf")
        .storageClass(StorageClass.STANDARD)
        .build(), somePath);
```

The storage classes differ by access pattern and cost. S3 Standard is for frequently accessed data with low latency and high throughput, stored across at least three Availability Zones. S3 Intelligent-Tiering is for data with unknown or changing access patterns; it monitors each object and automatically moves it between a frequent tier and infrequent tiers, charging a tiny monitoring fee but saving you from guessing. S3 Standard-IA (Infrequent Access) is cheaper to store but charges a per-gigabyte retrieval fee, suited to data accessed a few times a year, still across three Availability Zones. S3 One Zone-IA is like Standard-IA but stored in a single Availability Zone, so it is cheaper but will be lost if that zone is destroyed, which is acceptable only for reproducible data.

The Glacier classes are archival. S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval stores rarely accessed data but still gives millisecond retrieval, good for medical images or compliance records that must be instantly available when needed. S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval is cheaper and retrieves in minutes to hours. S3 Glacier Deep Archive is the cheapest of all, for data retained seven to ten years for regulation, retrieved in up to twelve hours.

A lifecycle policy automates movement between classes. You attach rules to a bucket (optionally scoped to a prefix or tag) that say, for example, transition objects to Standard-IA after thirty days, to Glacier Flexible Retrieval after ninety days, to Deep Archive after one year, and expire (permanently delete) after ten years. The rules also clean up incomplete multipart uploads and expire old object versions.

A senior nuance: transitions have minimums and small per-object transition charges, so transitioning millions of tiny objects too early can cost more than it saves. For unpredictable access, Intelligent-Tiering is safer than hand-written transition rules because it reacts to real usage rather than a fixed age guess.

