sections.s2_technical:
The first and most important control is S3 Block Public Access. It is a set of four settings at the account and bucket level that override any policy or ACL that would make objects public. For a bank, this should be on at the account level so no team can accidentally expose a bucket, even if someone writes a bad policy.

Next are access controls. IAM identity policies grant permissions to users and roles, while bucket policies are attached to the bucket and can grant or deny access to specific principals. You write them with least privilege, allowing only the exact actions and the exact resource.

```java
// A least-privilege bucket policy expressed as JSON text in Java
String policy = """
    {
      "Version": "2012-10-17",
      "Statement": [{
        "Effect": "Allow",
        "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/statement-service"},
        "Action": ["s3:GetObject"],
        "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::nbi-statements/*"
      },{
        "Effect": "Deny",
        "Principal": "*",
        "Action": "s3:*",
        "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::nbi-statements/*",
        "Condition": {"Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "false"}}
      }]
    }
    """;
```

The second statement above denies any request that is not over TLS, forcing encryption in transit.

Encryption at rest protects the stored bytes. SSE-S3 uses keys S3 manages for you, which is simple and free. SSE-KMS uses AWS Key Management Service keys, which gives you key rotation, fine-grained key policies, and an audit trail of every key use in CloudTrail — the right choice for regulated banking data. You can require encryption by rejecting unencrypted uploads via a bucket policy condition.

A VPC gateway endpoint for S3 keeps traffic private. Without it, an EC2 instance in a private subnet reaches S3 over the public internet via a NAT gateway. With a gateway endpoint, the traffic stays on the AWS network and never touches the internet, and you can even restrict the bucket to only accept requests coming through that endpoint.

Access logging and CloudTrail data events give you the audit trail: who accessed which object, when, and from where. For a bank this is essential for compliance and for investigating any incident.

The reason public buckets cause so many breaches is that S3 is used to store huge amounts of sensitive data, and making a bucket public requires only one wrong setting or ACL. Once public, every object is reachable by anyone on the internet with just the URL, no credentials needed, and automated scanners constantly crawl for open buckets. A senior nuance: the defense in depth matters because any single control can be misconfigured, so Block Public Access at the account level acts as the backstop that cannot be overridden by a careless bucket policy.

