sections.s2_technical:
EC2 gives you a virtual server. You pick the instance type, the operating system, and you are responsible for everything above the hypervisor: patching the OS, installing runtimes, configuring auto scaling groups, and securing the host. It is the right choice when you need full control, a specific operating system or kernel tuning, licensed software tied to a host, or a long-running process that does not fit a container model cleanly. The trade-off is operational burden.

ECS runs containers. You package the application as a Docker image, define a task definition (CPU, memory, image, environment, IAM task role), and ECS schedules and keeps the desired number of tasks running, integrating tightly with load balancers, IAM, CloudWatch, and VPC networking. It has a gentle learning curve and little control-plane overhead because AWS runs the orchestration. You choose ECS when your team wants containers with minimal operational complexity and is happy to stay within AWS.

```java
// A task definition idea expressed as simple config for clarity
record TaskDefinition(String image, int cpu, int memoryMb, String taskRoleArn) {}
TaskDefinition transfer = new TaskDefinition(
    "123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/transfer-service:1.4.0",
    512, 1024, "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/transfer-task-role");
```

EKS runs Kubernetes, managed by AWS for the control plane while you run workloads on worker nodes or Fargate. You get the full Kubernetes API, Helm charts, operators, and portability across clouds and on-premises, plus a huge ecosystem. The cost is complexity: you manage Kubernetes concepts, upgrades, and add-ons, and you usually need engineers who know Kubernetes. You choose EKS when you need Kubernetes portability, already have Kubernetes expertise and tooling, or must avoid cloud lock-in.

Lambda runs functions. You upload code, choose memory (which also scales CPU), and connect triggers such as API Gateway, S3 events, SQS, or EventBridge. AWS runs it only when invoked, scales it automatically from zero to thousands of concurrent executions, and bills per millisecond of execution. You choose Lambda for short, event-driven, spiky, or infrequent tasks — processing an uploaded file, reacting to a queue message, a lightweight API — where you want zero server management and to pay nothing when idle.

```java
// A Lambda handler reacting to an event (conceptual signature)
public class StatementUploadHandler {
    public String handleRequest(Map<String,Object> s3Event, Object context) {
        // triggered when a statement PDF lands in S3; index it, notify customer
        return "processed";
    }
}
```

A senior nuance: these are a spectrum of control versus management. EC2 is most control and most work; Lambda is least work and least control, with real constraints like a fifteen-minute maximum runtime and cold starts. ECS and EKS sit in between, and Fargate removes server management from both. I pick the least-operational option that still fits the workload's shape — long-running and heavy leans toward EC2 or containers, short and bursty leans toward Lambda, and the choice between ECS and EKS is usually about whether Kubernetes portability is worth its complexity.

